[
    {
        "content": "<p>In a Lean program I have two tuples of type <code>Nat × Nat</code> that I would like to compare lexicographically.  I was slightly surprised that I couldn't just compare them using <code>&lt;</code> as would work in e.g. Haskell or even Python:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>produces</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">synthesize</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">class</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">LT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>So I wrote a helper function:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pair_less</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y1</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y2</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">||</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&amp;&amp;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y2</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>But that seems like it should be unnecessary.  Is there some nicer/canonical way to perform this comparison?</p>",
        "id": 623128472,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1789042389
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Prod.Lex#doc\">docs#Prod.Lex</a></p>",
        "id": 623130898,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1789042914
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's not an instance because it's unclear whether one should choose the product order or the lexicographic one.</p>",
        "id": 623131438,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1789043042
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you are using mathlib, there is also <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Lex#doc\">docs#Lex</a> which creates a type synonym for the purposes of giving it the order instance, and there's also <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Prod.Lex.instLE#doc\">docs#Prod.Lex.instLE</a> which has <code>A ×ₗ B</code> as notation for <code>Lex (Prod A B)</code> (a product type with the Lexicographic order automatic)</p>",
        "id": 623132877,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1789043349
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I had noticed Prod.Lex in the docs, but I don't see how to use it to perform the comparison.  I tried this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>But I get</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Application</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mismatch</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">The</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">argument</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x2</span>\n<span class=\"n\">has</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">type</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">8</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">8</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span>\n<span class=\"n\">but</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">expected</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">type</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">WellFoundedRelation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">2</span>\n<span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">application</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x2</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 623133843,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1789043521
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Capital <code>L</code> in <code>Prod.Lex</code>?</p>",
        "id": 623134504,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1789043684
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, OK, that works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Lex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Thanks.  I also just tried to instantiate the LT type class so that I can get these comparisons automatically:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">instLT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Lex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>But afterward this fails:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>produces</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">synthesize</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Decidable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Any idea about how to fix this?</p>",
        "id": 623135759,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1789043983
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You also need</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecidableLT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstanceAs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">DecidableRel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Lex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 623139340,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1789044717
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>An alternative way in which you can do that is by defining an infix operator:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">prodLess</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Prod.Lex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">infixl</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"mi\">65</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\" &lt;ₗ \"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">prodLess</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;ₗ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>if you don't need to define a <code>LT</code> instance</p>",
        "id": 623139663,
        "sender_full_name": "David Mazarro",
        "timestamp": 1789044788
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Great, thanks for both helpful suggestions!</p>",
        "id": 623141480,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1789045188
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can also use <code>toLex (3, 4) &lt; toLex (3, 6)</code></p>",
        "id": 623157594,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1789048328
